(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion that has already reaccumulated once after drainage. Which consult is recommended to improve her outcomes and quality of life?","choices":["Endocrinology","Palliative care","Physical therapy","Rheumatology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy (median 2 to 4 months) and often recurs; a palliative care consult is recommended to improve outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Anakinra plus prednisone at once","Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more","Ibuprofen with colchicine, stopped at 2 weeks","Prednisone alone for 6 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is a first recurrence, symptoms returning after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had 10 days of gradually worsening pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain without a clear onset. His temperature is 38.7 degrees Celsius (101.7 degrees Fahrenheit), and the echocardiogram shows a moderate pericardial effusion without collapse of any chamber. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance and repeat echo in 3 months","Immediate pericardiectomy","Hospital admission for workup","Outpatient ibuprofen and colchicine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever above 38 degrees Celsius, a subacute course over days to weeks and a moderate to large effusion are all high-risk features that favor hospitalization and further evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has needed prednisone for several months for pericarditis that did not respond to first-line therapy. Which complication of her treatment should be specifically anticipated?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia","Hearing loss","Osteoporosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase the risk of osteoporosis, a particular concern in older adults on prolonged courses, so bone health must be addressed. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with acute idiopathic pericarditis after a viral illness. He is afebrile, his troponin is normal, the echocardiogram shows no effusion, and he has no contraindication to any medication. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Ibuprofen plus colchicine","Prednisone plus colchicine","Anakinra plus colchicine","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen plus colchicine is the initial recommended therapy for most acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis, with gastrointestinal protection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["Under 1%","About 15 to 30%","About 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after acute pericarditis, which is why completing the full course and recognizing symptoms early matter. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a new moderate pericardial effusion with joint aches and fatigue. She has not traveled and has no recent viral illness. Which test is most reasonable to add to her workup?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody","Blood cultures","Serum lipase","Hemoglobin A1c"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The effusion workup includes blood counts, chemistry, thyroid-stimulating hormone and troponins, and possibly an antinuclear antibody, for example in a young woman with an effusion, to look for lupus. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is discharged after a large viral pericardial effusion was found without tamponade. He asks how the effusion will be monitored. Which follow-up plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Serial transthoracic echocardiograms","Monthly electrocardiograms alone","Serial chest radiographs alone","No follow-up once symptoms ease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All large pericardial effusions should be followed with serial transthoracic echocardiograms, with close clinical follow-up by the specialty matching the cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman had a large effusion drained for tamponade. Afterward her jugular venous pressure remains elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), and she remains fluid overloaded. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Effusive-constrictive pericarditis","Recurrent acute pericarditis","Simple pericardial effusion","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Effusive-constrictive pericarditis is underlying constriction with a coexisting effusion, usually with tamponade, in which constriction persists after the effusion is drained. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The pericardium","The mitral valve","The chest wall","The pleura"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pleural rub is tied to respiration, heard in the inspiratory phase, and disappears with breath-holding; a pericardial rub persists, and even intensifies, while the breath is held. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man who recently moved from a region where tuberculosis is common has effusive pericarditis. Pericardial fluid is positive on acid-fast stain. Which treatment may significantly reduce his progression to constriction?","choices":["Antituberculous therapy","Early pericardiectomy","Long-term loop diuretic","Indefinite aspirin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If tuberculosis is the underlying etiology, treatment of the tuberculosis may significantly reduce progression to constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 67-year-old former smoker with weight loss has a large pericardial effusion. Lung cancer is suspected, and pericardiocentesis is performed. Which study of the pericardial fluid best addresses this suspicion?","choices":["Gram stain","Fungal culture","Cell count","Cytology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial fluid is sent for cell count, Gram stain, cultures, acid-fast studies and cytology, and cytology is the study that identifies malignant cells. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with advanced constrictive pericarditis and moderate biatrial enlargement reports new palpitations. His pulse is irregularly irregular. Which rhythm is he most prone to?","choices":["Complete heart block","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in advanced constrictive pericarditis, encouraged by biatrial enlargement; nonspecific ST and T-wave changes, tachycardia and low voltage are the other findings. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with tamponade from a newly found effusion and no known cancer is being evaluated. Which consideration about the cause is most important at her age?","choices":["Dressler syndrome is most likely","Her age makes the cause irrelevant","A higher suspicion for malignancy","Coxsackievirus is the likely cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Older adults with an effusion or tamponade warrant a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, while younger patients are more often idiopathic or viral. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man had a tuberculous pericardial effusion drained in the hospital and is starting antituberculous therapy. Which specialty should be part of his close follow-up?","choices":["Infectious disease","Nephrology","Rheumatology","Medical oncology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Close clinical follow-up is tailored to the underlying etiology: cardiology, infectious disease, nephrology, oncology or rheumatology; a tuberculous effusion calls for infectious disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with acute pericarditis is starting ibuprofen and colchicine. He has a history of dyspepsia. Which addition to his regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["An oral anticoagulant","A prophylactic antibiotic","A loop diuretic","A proton pump inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal protection is provided with anti-inflammatory therapy, for example a proton pump inhibitor, because these drugs reduce the stomach's protective prostaglandins. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner is diagnosed with acute viral pericarditis and starts ibuprofen and colchicine. He asks when he can return to training. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Run on alternate days during treatment","Avoid exercise for the rest of his life","Avoid strenuous activity for at least 1 month","Resume running once the chest pain stops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous activity should be avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, at least 1 month after diagnosis, keeping the maximal heart rate under 100. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Cardiology","Infectious disease","Gastroenterology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Referral to cardiology is advised for recurrences (recurrent pericarditis), for no response to conservative management and for the development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with constrictive pericarditis is scheduled for pericardiectomy next week. He takes a loop diuretic for leg edema. How should the diuretic be handled before surgery?","choices":["Add a second diuretic","Use it sparingly","Double the dose","Give it intravenously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If surgery is planned, diuretics should be used sparingly in the preoperative period, even though they relieve the fluid overload of constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic features of constriction are increased pericardial thickness, abnormal septal motion or bounce toward the left ventricle, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is readmitted 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery with fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). His sternal wound looks clean. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dressler syndrome","Sternal wound infection","Acute graft occlusion","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis weeks after myocardial infarction or post-cardiotomy (cardiac surgery) is Dressler syndrome, an inflammatory reaction to the injured tissue; aspirin plus colchicine treats it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman is hypotensive with distended neck veins and a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. After pericardiocentesis removes 600 mL, her blood pressure rises from 80/50 to 118/74 and her dyspnea resolves. What does this response establish?","choices":["It confirms constrictive disease","It shows the echo was misread","It confirms the tamponade diagnosis","It rules out a malignant cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tamponade is a clinical diagnosis; echocardiography identifies the effusion and its significance, but the diagnosis is confirmed only by the hemodynamic and clinical response to drainage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 8 days for presumed viral pericarditis. She still has a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius and pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pain, and a new pericardial effusion has appeared on echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop colchicine and add a diuretic","Start anakinra as a first step","Admit and look for another cause","Continue the same drugs for a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Persistent fever and pain with a new effusion define treatment failure, and failure to respond within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral; no improvement after 7 days favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has fatigue, exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), ascites and minimal ankle edema. His liver enzymes are mildly raised. Which condition is the key noncardiac mimic to consider?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Cirrhosis","Asthma","Pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis is in the differential of constrictive pericarditis because both cause ascites and fluid overload; an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign points back to the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, three-part rubbing sound) at the left sternal border. Which drug should be avoided in treating his pericarditis?","choices":["Aspirin","Colchicine","Pantoprazole","Ibuprofen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is post-infarction (Dressler) pericarditis, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen are not given after a myocardial infarction; glucocorticoids are also avoided. Aspirin plus colchicine is used. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man who grew up in a country where tuberculosis is common has fever, night sweats and a moderate pericardial effusion. Fluid is obtained by pericardiocentesis. Which fluid studies best address the suspected cause?","choices":["Gram stain and routine culture","Antinuclear antibody on the fluid","Acid-fast stain and mycobacterial culture","Cytology for malignant cells alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial fluid is sent for an acid-fast bacilli stain and mycobacterial culture to identify tuberculosis, alongside cell count, Gram stain, cultures and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Dressler syndrome","Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade, and his weight loss without a viral prodrome reinforces it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman treated for breast cancer 2 years ago has 3 weeks of fatigue and exertional dyspnea. Her blood pressure is 128/80 without hypotension. The chest radiograph shows an enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette with clear lung fields. Which test is required to confirm a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Repeat chest radiograph","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Coronary angiography","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical evaluation, electrocardiogram and chest radiograph may suggest a pericardial effusion, but an echocardiogram is required to confirm it and to assess its hemodynamic significance. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with renal failure has a large pericardial effusion and borderline blood pressure. His chest radiograph shows a markedly enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette. What do his lung fields most likely show?","choices":["Lobar consolidation","Clear on both sides","Bat-wing pulmonary edema","Bilateral hilar adenopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A large effusion gives cardiomegaly with a globular, water-bottle silhouette, and the lung fields may be clear, unlike the congestion of heart failure. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man comes to a primary care office with dyspnea, lightheadedness, distended neck veins and a blood pressure of 86/58. He was diagnosed with a pericardial effusion last month. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Schedule cardiology in two weeks","Send him to the emergency department","Order an outpatient echocardiogram","Start ibuprofen and colchicine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evidence suggesting cardiac tamponade is a high-risk feature that requires hospitalization, and tamponade is an emergency, so he goes to the emergency department now. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
